+## Editing an existing source model
+
+When the task supplies a GLB to edit, the construction subject below is ONLY the
+new/replacement part. Retain all other source geometry, materials, node names and
+world transforms unchanged. Do not independently reconstruct the host. This is
+an editing scope selection, not a relaxation of the part-construction pipeline.
+Read `docs/edit_render_backend.md` for this branch's capture adapter; all build,
+review, camera iteration and gate requirements below otherwise remain shared.
+
+For every turn, read the text instruction AND target image; inspect the current
+model and infer the operation and affected parts yourself. Start from your own
+previous saved output (turn 1 starts from the supplied source). Do not reset to
+GT, use external assets or obtain hidden GT geometry/diagnostic channels.
+
+- Remove: physically delete the identified target and its exclusively owned data,
+  compact/remap GLB references, reload and verify preservation of surviving parts.
+  Do not merely hide or unlink scene.nodes. Render if uncertain; no construction
+  spec or factory is required when no new part is built.
+- Add/replace: apply the COMPLETE original assessment/spec/factory/locked-pass/
+  gate/review/correction workflow below to the new part. Install it in the retained
+  source and inspect both part geometry and assembly. Preserve unrelated children.
+- Retexture: directly edit the named part's material, texture, or shader parameters
+  to match the instruction and target appearance. Preserve its geometry, topology,
+  UV coordinates, node names, transforms, placement, and attachment relationships.
+  If materials/textures are shared, isolate the target's material assignment or
+  texture edits so unrelated parts remain unchanged. Record semantic_op=retexture,
+  execution_op=material_edit. Do not rebuild or replace the part for a material-only
+  instruction; no new-part factory or geometry-construction passes are required.
+  Retain the applicable material/render review and bounded correction loop: render,
+  compare with target RGB, revise the material, and record evidence and gate results.
+  Use the same semantic/six-pass and diagnostic-view mechanisms where applicable;
+  verify unchanged geometry and untouched regions, and return to the evaluation
+  camera for final review. Mark construction-only checks not applicable with a
+  reason, not passed; missing target diagnostic channels remain unavailable.
+
+Keep one construction state/spec per edited part/turn. The source/current GLB is
+an unchanged-region baseline, not the target for the requested change. Collect
+six-pass evidence and real semantic mappings using the branch adapter; compare
+unchanged regions against your own previous model, requested appearance against
+supplied target RGB. Without target diagnostic channels, paired target-channel
+comparison is unavailable, not a pass. Never fabricate channels or gate evidence.
+
+Retain original camera-first iteration and freely chosen diagnostic/close-up views.
+When an evaluation camera is supplied, preserve it separately and return to it
+before accepting a constructed turn. Do not substitute a fitted camera for final
+scoring. Run the same gates and bounded correction budgets in either backend.
+### Bounded editing and best-candidate delivery
+
+For unattended editing chains, every turn must attempt the requested actual edit.
+Do not satisfy a turn by copying the previous GLB, changing only serialization or
+metadata, or making an unrelated cosmetic change. For a remove whose target is
+already absent, report that limitation honestly; do not delete a different part.
+
+Preserve the finite correction budget: at most 3 corrections per applicable pass
+and 6 total per turn (material-only edits also use the 6-total bound). Never reset
+or evade counters. Save each runnable edited candidate before further corrections.
+Judge the best candidate by instruction fulfillment and target-view agreement,
+then preservation of untouched content; record its path, hashes and selection reason.
+
+A deliverable candidate is a complete GLB that reloads, has finite geometry and
+valid scene references, preserves unrelated source content, and contains the
+requested kind of edit. This is export validity, NOT a claim that quality gates pass.
+At budget exhaustion or a quality hard stop, export the best such EDITED candidate
+as output/turnNN.glb, mark it partial if any required gate failed or is unavailable,
+and use that saved candidate as the next turn's input. Never discard an available
+edited candidate merely to restore the unedited state because a quality gate failed.
+
+A pre-construction reference/material/spec gate rejection or a broken gate utility
+must not prevent all editing. Record the failure, attempt environment repair when
+applicable, and produce a bounded provisional procedural/material edit if no
+runnable edited candidate exists yet. This provisional attempt consumes the same
+turn budget; it is not a new pass, budget reset, or gate-approved factory build.
+If a strict generator refuses, retain its BLOCKED artifact; a directly authored
+provisional candidate must be labeled as such, not as a successful locked pass.
+Do not forge state, force a failed review to continue, or claim skipped gates passed.
+
+This is an explicit unattended EDITING override of reconstruction-only hard-stop,
+request-input and fail-closed delivery rules below: a hard stop still ends quality
+acceptance and further correction, but does not forbid delivery of a valid edited
+candidate or the bounded initial provisional attempt described above. Interactive
+reconstruction retains the original stop/request-input behavior.
+
+Only when no edited candidate can be exported and reloaded after the bounded
+attempts, record execution failure (failed, not done). Keep the prior state solely
+as a separately identified recovery checkpoint, not a successful output for that
+turn. Record dependent turns as blocked when no valid rollout input exists; never
+silently reset to GT or count copied prior states as completed edits.
+
+Export the complete edited scene as output/turnNN.glb, in source coordinates.
+Preserve source input/ref files and keep intermediate artifacts inside the example.
+GLB serialization/merging is authorized plumbing; new parts remain procedural
+Three.js code. Retained source content is not subject to the no-copy reconstruction
+rule. Animation/UI requirements apply to newly built parts when the task requires
+those capabilities, not as permission to alter the untouched host.
 
